# Helpy Cursor Rules

## Critical Patterns - DO NOT MODIFY

### Bottom Sheets - MUST BE ABOVE BOTTOM NAV

**RULE: Every bottom sheet/modal MUST use `createPortal` to render to `document.body`.**

This ensures bottom sheets ALWAYS appear above the bottom navigation bar, regardless of any CSS stacking contexts.

#### Option 1: Use the BottomSheet Component (Preferred)

```tsx
import BottomSheet from '@/components/ui/BottomSheet';

<BottomSheet isOpen={isOpen} onClose={() => setIsOpen(false)}>
  <BottomSheet.Header>
    <h2 className="text-title text-foreground">Title</h2>
  </BottomSheet.Header>
  <BottomSheet.Body>
    <p>Content</p>
  </BottomSheet.Body>
  <BottomSheet.Footer>
    <button>Action</button>
  </BottomSheet.Footer>
</BottomSheet>
```

#### Option 2: Manual createPortal (for custom modals)

```tsx
import { createPortal } from 'react-dom';

{isOpen && createPortal(
  <div className="fixed inset-0 bg-black/30 backdrop-blur-sm z-[60] flex items-end justify-center bottom-sheet-backdrop">
    {/* Safe area cover */}
    <div className="absolute bottom-0 left-0 right-0 bg-card" style={{ height: 'env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 34px)' }} />
    <div className="bg-card w-full max-w-lg rounded-t-2xl overflow-hidden bottom-sheet-content" style={{ marginBottom: 'env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 34px)' }}>
      {/* Content */}
    </div>
  </div>
, document.body)}
```

#### Why This Matters

- CSS `animation` and `opacity` properties create stacking contexts
- Elements inside a stacking context cannot escape it with z-index
- The Layout component wraps page content, creating potential stacking issues
- `createPortal` renders directly to `document.body`, bypassing all stacking contexts

#### Z-Index Hierarchy

| Element | Z-Index |
|---------|---------|
| Bottom Navigation | `z-10` |
| Sticky Headers | `z-20` |
| Dropdowns | `z-50` |
| **Bottom Sheets/Modals** | `z-[60]` + `createPortal` |
| `.bottom-sheet-backdrop` CSS | `z-index: 9999 !important` |

**NEVER create a bottom sheet without `createPortal` or the `BottomSheet` component!**

---

### Meals Page - LOCKED PATTERN (DO NOT MODIFY)

**The Meals page uses fixed headers + container scrolling. DO NOT CHANGE.**

#### Fixed Headers (Meals.tsx)
- Header: `position: fixed; top: 0` with `paddingTop: env(safe-area-inset-top)`
- Week nav: `position: fixed` with `top: calc(env(safe-area-inset-top) + 120px)`
- Both have `touchAction: 'none'` to prevent scroll on touch
- Shadow on week nav controlled by `isContainerScrolled` state

**Why Fixed Instead of Sticky:**
- `position: fixed` is relative to the **viewport**, not scroll container
- Immune to iOS Safari stacking context and layout bugs
- Never moves regardless of scroll position or touch behavior

**DO NOT** change headers to sticky positioning.

#### Content Area (Meals.tsx)
- Has `paddingTop: calc(env(safe-area-inset-top) + 208px)` for fixed headers
- Outer container: `h-screen bg-background overflow-hidden` (locked to viewport)
- Day view: `overflow-y-auto` with `touchAction: 'pan-y'` (vertical only)
- Week view: `overflow-auto` with `touchAction: 'pan-x pan-y'` (both directions)

**DO NOT** change the outer container from `h-screen overflow-hidden`.

#### Always-Mounted Pattern (App.tsx)
- Meals is rendered OUTSIDE Layout's keyed container
- Uses `display: block/none` based on activeView
- Passes `isActive={activeView === 'meals'}` prop

**DO NOT** move Meals back into renderView() or add key={} to its wrapper.

#### Layout Fragment Pattern (Layout.tsx)
- When children is null (Meals active), wrapper div is not rendered
- Only bottom nav is rendered, preventing empty 80px div above Meals

**DO NOT** change back to always-render wrapper pattern.

#### Scroll-to-Today Logic (Meals.tsx)
- Uses `getBoundingClientRect()` for accurate container-relative positioning
- Uses `useLayoutEffect` with single `requestAnimationFrame` for proper timing
- `hasInitiallyScrolled` ref prevents duplicate scrolls

**DO NOT** change to `offsetTop` or window scroll.

See `docs/MEALS_SCROLL_FIX.md` for full details.

#### View Switch Flicker Prevention (Meals.tsx) - LOCKED PATTERN

**The Meals page uses specific patterns to prevent visual flicker when switching between day/week views.**

##### Problem: display:none → block Transition
- Meals component is always mounted but hidden with `display: none` when not active
- When becoming visible, `getBoundingClientRect()` needs one frame to return accurate values
- Without protection, users see content at wrong scroll position for one frame

##### Solution: visibility + requestAnimationFrame + isScrollReady State

```tsx
const [isScrollReady, setIsScrollReady] = useState(false);

useLayoutEffect(() => {
  if (!isActive) {
    setIsScrollReady(false);
    return;
  }
  if (hasInitiallyScrolled.current) {
    setIsScrollReady(true);
    return;
  }
  
  // Single rAF to wait for display:none → block layout
  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    // ... scroll logic ...
    hasInitiallyScrolled.current = true;
    setIsScrollReady(true);
  });
}, [view, isActive]);

// Container hidden until scroll ready
<div style={{ visibility: isScrollReady ? 'visible' : 'hidden' }}>
```

**Why This Works:**
1. Container starts hidden (`visibility: hidden`)
2. `requestAnimationFrame` waits for browser to calculate layout
3. Scroll executes at correct position
4. `isScrollReady` reveals container with content already positioned
5. All happens in ~16ms (one frame), imperceptible to user

**DO NOT** remove `isScrollReady` state or `visibility` control.
**DO NOT** change `requestAnimationFrame` to `setTimeout`.
**DO NOT** use `useEffect` instead of `useLayoutEffect` for this logic.

#### Week Nav Shadow (Meals.tsx) - LOCKED PATTERN

**The shadow under week nav MUST be conditional on view type.**

```tsx
// CORRECT - shadow only shows in day view
boxShadow: (view === 'day' && isContainerScrolled) ? '0 8px 16px -8px rgba(0,0,0,0.15)' : 'none'

// WRONG - causes flicker on view switch (stale state shows shadow on table view)
boxShadow: isContainerScrolled ? '...' : 'none'
```

**Why This Pattern:**
- When switching from day (scrolled) to table, `isContainerScrolled` state is still `true` for one frame
- By checking `view === 'day'` in the render, shadow never appears on table view regardless of state timing
- No CSS transition on shadow - changes are instant to prevent delayed show/hide

**DO NOT** add `transition-shadow` class to week nav.
**DO NOT** remove the `view === 'day'` check from the shadow condition.

#### Meals Table View (Week View) - LOCKED PATTERN

**The Meals table view has specific patterns for sticky headers/columns and borders. DO NOT CHANGE.**

##### Scroll Containment
- Table is inside a scroll container with `overflow-auto`
- Parent containers have `touchAction: 'none'` to prevent page scroll
- Table container has `touchAction: 'pan-x pan-y'` for horizontal/vertical scroll within table only
- Auto-scroll uses `weekScrollRef.current.scrollTo()` - **NEVER use `window.scrollTo()`**

##### Sticky Header Row (thead)
- `position: sticky; top: 0; zIndex: 10` on `<thead>`
- Header cells have `border-t border-b border-border` via Tailwind
- **Table has NO top border** - header cells provide their own top border so it stays visible when scrolling

##### Sticky First Column (Date Column)
- Date cells and corner cell have `sticky left-0`
- Corner cell: `z-[15]` (above both header row and date column)
- Date cells: `z-[5]`
- First column cells have `borderLeft` via **inline styles** (Tailwind `bg-*` classes break borders)
- **Table has NO left border** - first column cells provide their own left border

##### Border Pattern - CRITICAL
| Element | Border Method | Why |
|---------|---------------|-----|
| Table outer | Inline style: `borderRight`, `borderBottom` only | Top/left provided by cells |
| Header cells | Tailwind: `border-t border-b border-r border-border` | Works with `bg-muted` |
| Corner cell | Tailwind + Inline: `border-t border-b border-r` + inline `borderLeft` | Left via inline to work with bg |
| Date cells | Tailwind + Inline: `border-r border-b` + inline `borderLeft`, `backgroundColor` | **Background MUST be inline** |
| Content cells | Tailwind + Inline: `border-r border-b` + inline `backgroundColor` | **Background MUST be inline** |
| Breakfast column | Additional inline: `borderLeft: 'none'` | Prevents double border with date column |

##### Why Inline Styles for Backgrounds?
**Tailwind `bg-card` and `bg-primary` classes interfere with Tailwind border classes.**
When you add `bg-card` via Tailwind, borders stop rendering. Solution: use inline `backgroundColor` instead.

```tsx
// WRONG - borders will break
className="border-b border-border bg-card"

// CORRECT - borders work
className="border-b border-border"
style={{ backgroundColor: 'hsl(var(--card))' }}
```

##### No Box-Shadow for Borders
**DO NOT use `boxShadow` to create visual borders.** It causes double/thick borders when combined with real borders.

##### Table Structure Summary
```
<table style={{ borderRight, borderBottom, marginBottom: 160px }}>
  <thead style={{ position: sticky, top: 0, zIndex: 10 }}>
    <tr>
      <th>Corner - sticky left-0 z-[15], border-t/b/r + inline borderLeft</th>
      <th>Breakfast - border-t/b/r</th>
      <th>Lunch - border-t/b/r</th>
      <th>Dinner - border-t/b/r</th>
      <th>Snacks - border-t/b (no border-r, table provides)</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Date - sticky left-0, inline borderLeft + backgroundColor</td>
      <td>Breakfast - inline backgroundColor, borderLeft: none</td>
      <td>Lunch - inline backgroundColor</td>
      <td>Dinner - inline backgroundColor</td>
      <td>Snacks - inline backgroundColor</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

---

## Code Style

- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
- Follow existing component patterns
- Keep translations in the `t` object
- Use `useScrollHeader` hook for sticky header animations
- Use `useScrollLock` when modals are open
